  "phenomenon": "You are in class studying at night and one of the fluorescent tubes blows out! Why do the other tubes remain lit? Students also observe that three identical bulbs connected one after another (in series) to a 9 V battery light dimmer than identical bulbs connected in parallel to an identical battery. In a Kenyan school or home, such as a boarding school dormitory in Kericho or a classroom in Nairobi, the lights keep working when one tube fails, yet when one bulb in an old string of Christmas lights fails, the whole string may go dark. The puzzle is that the same type of battery and the same bulbs give very different brightness and behaviour depending only on how they are connected. Why does the connection pattern change brightness and what happens when one component fails?",
