        "teacherMoves": "Say: \"Watch carefully. Your job now is to be a scientist: describe exactly what you see, without explaining it yet.\" Connect Demo A and give WAIT TIME of 10-15 seconds in silence while learners observe. Ask: \"What do you notice about the brightness of each bulb?\" Cold-call 3 learners. Then say: \"I will now unscrew the middle bulb. What do you predict will happen? Write it down.\" Wait 10 seconds, then unscrew it. Ask 2 cold-calls: \"What did you see? Was it what you predicted?\" Repeat for Demo B. Keep the same bulbs and the same pack, and say aloud: \"Notice that nothing has changed except how the wires are arranged.\" Do not name or use the words 'current', 'voltage' or 'resistance' as explanations, and if learners use them, say: \"Hold that thought, we will put it on our board as something to find out.\" Circulate with the question: \"What evidence tells you that?\" Ask groups to compare the two circuits for 3 minutes using sentence frames: 'In the series circuit... but in the parallel circuit...'. Show the Christmas-light string and ask: \"Which of our two circuits does this string behave like? Which does the Nairobi classroom behave like?\" Take 3 cold-calls. Switch off the bulbs between demos so the filaments and the pack stay cool.",
